<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.8.2: Recognizing the Expanded Product of the Difference of Two Squares</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02214</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.8.2: Recognizing the Expanded Product of the Difference of Two Squares</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e81e8d2a-8bd7-454d-9c2c-60a1c3a93942</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Clare claims that \((10 + 3)(10 - 3)\) is equivalent to \(10^2-3^2\) and \((20 + 1)(20 - 1)\) is equivalent to \(20^2-1^2\). Do you agree? Show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer and your reasoning.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Agree.</para>
    
    <para>\((10+3)(10-3)\)
      \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;&amp;{=\left(10+3\right)\left(10+(-3)\right)\\=10^2+10\left(-3\right)+3\left(10\right)+3\left(-3\right)\\=10^2-30+30-3^2\\=10^2-3^2}\\&amp;&amp;\end{array}\)</para>
    
    <para>\((20 + 1)(20 - 1)\)
      \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;&amp;{=\left(20 + (-1)\right)\left(20 -1\right)\\= 20^2+ 20\left(-1\right) + 20\left(1\right) + 1\left(-1\right) \\= 20^2- 12}\\&amp;&amp;\end{array}\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
  <item>You can use your observations from the first question to mentally solve an expression such as \(105 \cdot 95\).</item>
</list>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>First, think about the problem differently. Evaluate \((100 + 5)(100 - 5)\) using the distributive property, or FOIL. Show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer and your reasoning.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\((100 + 5)(100 - 5) = 100^2- 5^2= 10,000 - 25 = 9975\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Check your answer by computing \(105 \cdot 95\).</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(105 \cdot 95 = 9975\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Is \((x + 4)(x - 4)\) equivalent to \(x^2-4^2\)?</para>
    <para>First, support your thinking using a diagram.</para>
    <table class="os-raise-doubleheadertable">
      
      <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis/></entry><entry><emphasis>
            \(x\)
         </emphasis> </entry><entry><emphasis>
            4
         </emphasis> </entry></row><row>
          <entry><emphasis>
          \(x\)
         </emphasis> </entry><entry> </entry>
          <entry> </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>
            -4
         </emphasis> </entry><entry> </entry>
          <entry> </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    
  
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>Yes.</para>
  
  <table class="os-raise-doubleheadertable">
    
    <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
        <entry><emphasis/></entry><entry><emphasis>
          \(x\)
       </emphasis> </entry><entry><emphasis>
         4
		 </emphasis> </entry></row><row>
        <entry><emphasis>
          \(x\)
       </emphasis> </entry><entry>
          \(x^2\)
        </entry>
        <entry>
        \(4x\)
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry><emphasis>
       -4
       </emphasis> </entry><entry>
          \(-4x\)
        </entry>
        <entry>
         \(-4(4)\)
        </entry>
      </row>
    </tbody></tgroup></table>
  
  

</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Now, support your thinking using the distributive property, or FOIL.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\((x + 4)(x - 4)= x^2+ 4x - 4x - 4(4) = x^2- 4^2\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Is \((x + 4)^2\) equivalent to \(x^2+4^2\)? Support your answer, either with or without a diagram.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>No.
    With a diagram:</para>
  
  <table class="os-raise-doubleheadertable">
    
    <tgroup cols="3"><tbody><row>
        <entry><emphasis/></entry><entry><emphasis>
          \(x\)
       </emphasis> </entry><entry><emphasis>
          4
       </emphasis> </entry></row><row>
        <entry><emphasis>
         \(x\)
       </emphasis> </entry><entry>
          \(x^2\)
        </entry>
        <entry>
        \(4x\)
			
        </entry>
      </row>
      <row>
        <entry><emphasis>
          4
       </emphasis> </entry><entry>
         \(4x\)
        </entry>
        <entry>
        \(4(4)\)
        </entry>
      </row>
    </tbody></tgroup></table>
  
  
  
  <para>With FOIL:
    \((x + 4)^2= (x + 4)(x + 4) = x^2+4x +4x +4(4) = x^2+8x +16\)
    \(x^2+ 4^2 = x^2+ 16\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example>
<!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    
  
  
  
  </problem></exercise>
</example><!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
<exercise class="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
    
    <para>Explain how your work in the previous questions can help you mentally evaluate \(22 \cdot 18\).</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your explanation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(22 \cdot 18\) can be written as \((20 + 2)(20 - 2)\), which is equivalent to \(202 - 22\), which is \(400 - 4\), or 396.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->

<exercise class="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>Explain how your work in the previous questions can help you mentally evaluate \(45 \cdot 35\).</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your explanation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(45 \cdot 35\) can be written as \((40 + 5)(40 - 5)\), which is equivalent to \(40^2-5^2\), which is \(1600 - 25\), or 1575.</para>
    
    
  
  </solution></exercise>

<exercise class="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>Here is a shortcut that can be used to mentally square any two-digit number. Let's take \(83^2\), for example.</para>
    
    <para>83 is \(80 + 3\).</para>
      <para>Compute \(80^2\) and \(3^2\), which gives 6400 and 9. Add these values to get 6409.</para>
      <para>Compute \(80 \cdot 3\), which is 240. Double it to get 480.</para>
      <para>Add 6409 and 480 to get 6889.</para>
    <para>Try using this method to find the squares of some other two-digit numbers. (With some practice, it is possible to get really fast at this!)</para>
    
    <para>Explain why this method works.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your explanation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Any two-digit number can be written as \(a + b\), where \(a\) is 10 times the first digit and \(b\) is the second digit. We are interested in squaring \(a + b\):</para>
    
    <para>\((a + b)^2=(a + b)(a + b)\)</para>
    
    <para>\(= a^2+ab +ab +b^2\) by the distributive property</para>
    
    <para>\(= a^2+ 2ab +b^2\) by combining like terms</para>
    
    <para>\(= a^2+b^2+2ab\) because addition is commutative</para>
    
    
    
    <para>We get the \(a^2 + b^2\) from squaring \(a\) and \(b\) and adding them, and the \(2ab\) from multiplying the two values and doubling them.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction--></section>
